```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a misconfigured default gateway on pc_0_0. The host has a
statically (or incorrectly) configured default route pointing to
10.0.0.254 (via eth0), but no device in the 10.0.0.0/24 subnet owns
that address. The ARP table on pc_0_0 confirms this: 10.0.0.254 is in
FAILED/incomplete state, meaning every ARP request for the next-hop has
gone unanswered.

The correct gateway is leaf_router_0_0 eth2, which is configured as
10.0.0.1/24. pc_0_0 can still ping 10.0.0.1 because that destination
is on-link and ARP for it succeeds (the ARP cache shows 10.0.0.1 →
02:05:97:cb:bc:6e, STALE but usable). Any packet destined outside the
local /24, however, is handed to the broken default next-hop
10.0.0.254, where ARP resolution permanently fails and the packet is
silently dropped. The fabric itself — BGP sessions, spine/leaf
forwarding, leaf_router_0_0 routing — is completely healthy.

The "workstation maintenance task" almost certainly overwrote the
network configuration on pc_0_0 with a wrong gateway value (10.0.0.254
instead of 10.0.0.1).

Corrective fix: on pc_0_0, delete the stale default route and install
the correct one:
  ip route del default
  ip route add default via 10.0.0.1 dev eth0
For persistence, update /etc/network/interfaces (or the equivalent
startup script) to set the gateway to 10.0.0.1.
=== END ===
```